  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 117 PM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSurface high pressure is building into the region with upper-level \ntroughing exiting to the east. With an increasingly subsident \natmosphere in place, the widespread stratocumulus that blanketed the \narea has moved east into Ohio. Additionally, lingering boundary \nlayer moisture and excellent radiational cooling conditions lead to \npatchy fog this morning. Fog was largely concentrated around rivers \nand in agricultural regions.\n\nBoundary layer mixing has commenced since sunrise with temperatures \napproaching 50 as of 1pm. A few diurnal cumulus have developed which \nsignifies that boundary layer mixing is nearing its maximum. A few \nadditional degrees of warming are possible under mostly sunny skies, \nresulting in high temps between 50 and 53 degrees.\n\nExcellent radiational cooling potential remains in place overnight \ntonight. Temperatures rapidly drop after sunset falling into the 30s \nby Midnight. Temperatures then gradually drop into the upper 20s in \nrural areas and low 30s in urbanized areas. Like this morning, \npatchy fog appears likely given similar conditions. Fog is again \nlikely to be concentrated near rivers and in open agricultural \nareas. Fog may be locally dense at times.\n\nTUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY\n\nSurface high pressure remains in place through the remainder of the \nweek. Aloft, ridging over the Rockies amplifies and moves eastward. \nCombined with the surface high centered just to the east, inducing \nlight southerly surface flow...a warming trend is expected to take \nhold this week. High temperatures climb through the 50s and \neventually into the 60s by Wednesday. A few locations may reach 70 \non Friday and Saturday. Efficient boundary layer mixing may cause \ndew points and RH to be on the lower end of the probabilistic range \n(as low as the 25th percentile).\n\nLow temperatures will be on the cool side as radiative potential \nremains optimal. Lows climb as well with sub-freezing temperatures \nbecoming less common each night this week. Patchy fog may be present \nevery night in the aforementioned fog-prone locations.\n\nSATURDAY ONWARD\n\nSaturday continues the warming trend with highs in the 60s to near \n70. However, surface high pressure begins to lose its grip as a \nstrong surface low passes to our northwest. This system will drag a \ncold front through Indiana late Saturday night or early Sunday \nmorning. A few showers are possible with the front but overall the \nbest lift likely remains displaced well to the north. As such, \nshower activity should be isolated with relatively light amounts.\n\nAnother area of high pressure builds in behind the front on Sunday \nalong with a colder air mass. Temperatures return to near normal \n(upper 40s to near 50 for highs, low 30s for lows).",
